#
# prima-materia — the searches of the run on the charms of the Orenburg
# Cossacks, 2026-10-12
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:OrenburgZagovoryFailureSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether any item of the Orenburg collection names a sign or a term by which its failure would be known"@en, "Ob ein Stück der Orenburger Sammlung ein Zeichen oder eine Frist nennt, an der sein Misslingen zu erkennen wäre"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "A. I. Myakutin, Pesni orenburgskikh kazakov, vol. IV, St Petersburg 1910, sections VII and VIII complete and section IX items 5 and 6, read at the wording in the Russian of the printing."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A sign, a count, a term or a stated outcome by which the speaker or a bystander would know that a charm, a carried text or a rite had not worked, and any clause that says what follows from such a case."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Russian of the old orthography and its misreadings in the transcription: не поможетъ, не поможет, если не, буде не, аще не, когда не, не будетъ, дней, днемъ, разъ, разь, сорокъ, тридцать, тридевять, срокъ, every numeral and every conditional particle in the three sections, read in place."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-12" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-12" ;
    skos:note "Nothing, and the nothing is exact. The numbers in the three sections are thirty Amens, thirty poprishcha, thrice-nine locks, seventy-seven limbs, twelve crosses, twelve daughters of Herod, three bows, three bakings and forty alms in forty days; not one of them is a term after which an effect is due or a count at which the speaker would conclude it had failed. The only conditional clauses in the sections are in the letter from heaven and attach their consequence to disbelief and to not keeping Sunday, entered at pmc:NebesnyiListNeverie."@en ;
    skos:note "What the search found instead and did not look for: a clause in the fourth charm by which words forgotten in the speaking stand where they belong, entered at pmc:ZagovorZabytyeSlova. It removes the one condition of performance that a spoken charm would otherwise put in the speaker's own hands."@en .

pmp:OrenburgZagovoryConditionsSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the Orenburg charms state who may speak them, when, and on what conditions"@en, "Ob die Orenburger Sprüche sagen, wer sie sprechen darf, wann und unter welchen Bedingungen"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same sections of the same volume."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Conditions of speaker, time, place, silence or secrecy, of the kind the European folk handbooks in this holding attach to their procedures (pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicUnbeschrauen, pmc:EuropeanFolkMagicMondfenster), asked of the same kind of material from another region."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "утромъ, вечеромъ, зарей, на зарѣ, до восхода, натощакъ, пятница, новолуніе, мѣсяцъ, молча, никому, не сказывать, тайно, and the opening formulas of rising, washing and going out, read in place; the collector's footnotes read as well."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-12" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-12" ;
    skos:note "No condition of time, silence, secrecy or speaker is stated for any of the eleven spoken charms. The rising from sleep, washing and going out to the east that open four of them are part of the wording and are spoken as narrative, not given as instructions. The only fixed times in the sections belong to other items: midnight for driving out the domovoi, and the five Fridays of the apocryphal list. Whether this is a property of the charms or of the collector's printing — which gives the words and almost never the use — is not decided by this volume, and a collection that prints the use would decide it."@en .
